× Kansas City one of 7 cities that could win a ‘dream dog park’ makeover
KANSAS CITY, Mo. — Swope Park is one of seven spots across the country with a chance to win a ‘Dream Dog Park’ makeover from Wal-Mart and Purina Beneful. The $30,000 donation could be used towards several types of enhancement projects, including extra benches and tables, shade structures and landscaping and water features.
People can cast a vote by sharing the contest with friends. Each share equals one vote, and voting is open until Nov. 1. Voters can visit www.walmart.com/Beneful to vote and share.
As of Wednesday, Sept. 21, Swope Park only has about five percent of the vote. Indianapolis was in the lead with 46 percent.
The other cities/parks participating in the special promotion include:
Boomer Street Dog Park near Milwaukee, Wisconsin;
Broad Ripple Dog Park in Indianapolis, Indiana;
Leash Free Zone Dog Park near Dallas, Texas;
Millbrook Dog Park in Raleigh, North Carolina;
PAW Park in Oklahoma City, Oklahoma;
Piedmont Park Dog Park in Atlanta, Georgia;
Swope Dog Park in Kansas City, Missouri
The Beneful Dream Dog Park Project began six years ago. Beneful says it has provided more than $2 million in funding to build new dog parks and improve existing ones.

French Bulldog puppies seized from illegal smugglers at UK border
Leaving the EU offers an opportunity of a lifetime to introduce tough new pet travel regulations that stop the despicable trade of dogs into the UK stained by cruelty and corruption.
The Dogs Trust today reveals the shocking way British pet lovers are being exploited by crooked vets and dog breeders in Eastern Europ which is opening the doors for the spread of diseases such as rabies.
Its latest undercover operation – the fourth the charity has carried out to expose the overseas puppy trade – reveals how:
· Puppies are being sedated in cramped conditions and undergoing 30 hour journeys to the UK with little water and no toilet breaks;
· Heavily pregnant bitches caged in “sickening conditions” endure gruelling trips so their puppies can be classed as “UK-born” and get quicker sales;
· Corrupt overseas vets falsify paperwork and provide fake vaccination stamps to allow puppies to travel when they are younger than legally allowed and not protected from diseases;
· Some vets are prepared to supply sedatives to bring puppies into the UK without pet passports;
· A dealer boasting how he has 300 bitches producing “designer puppies” such as French bulldogs, some of which are advertised and sold in the UK;
· One Hungarian dealer boasts around 400 puppies are exported from his home town every week to Spain – creating a potential £28 million turnover;
· Puppies from Serbia are being sold with European Union microchips and pet passports to be passed off as being bred inside the EU for easier entry into western countries
Tiny Yorkies offered for sale to Dogs Trust undercover investigator in Hungary
The Dogs Trust is today calling on the Government to use Brexit as an opportunity to redraw UK pet travel rules to end the “cruel trade”. It says findings from the latest undercover investigation continue to expose gaping loopholes in the Pet Travel Scheme (PETS) and highlight how unscrupulous dealers are exploiting the UK’s fascination with breeds such as French Bulldogs, Pugs, Dachshunds and English Bulldogs.
Paula Boyden, veterinary director of Dogs Trust, said: “Our investigations have revealed shocking welfare conditions. There is no regard for the lives of these vulnerable puppies, who are subjected to gruelling journeys of more than 30 hours in hot, cramped and filthy conditions with no toilet breaks and very little water. Some don’t survive the journey, and those who do may have physical health and behavioural issues due to their poor start in life.
“Puppy smugglers are only concerned with making a profit, and the UK provides an attractive market because the high demand for ‘designer breeds’ converts into fast internet sales. Importers are exploiting the lack of visual checks being made at the borders, and insufficient penalties for illegally importing puppies mean there is no real deterrent for these abhorrent crimes.
“With Brexit around the corner, this is an opportunity of a lifetime to put robust measures in place that protect dogs and the public. We urge the Government to take forward our clear recommendations to overhaul our pet travel legislation and increase the penalties for those caught fuelling this despicable trade.”
The Dogs Trust is calling on the Government to increase penalties for anyone caught illegally importing dogs and introduce “punitive” fixed penalty notices.
It also wants to see a centrally accessible data base to log pets’ microchip details with dates of entry into the UK as well as introducing rules to ensure the welfare of transported dogs, which are currently regulated by the EU and have very few requirements to protect animals.
